WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.960
Wouldn't it be great to live in the world
of Star Wars?

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:06.780
Oh, sure, you might have to deal with random
incursions on your home planet by jerks in

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:07.860
fancy helmets.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:12.139
You might run afoul of some rough and tumble
characters at your local cantina — or you

00:00:12.139 --> 00:00:15.700
might be one of those characters, and end
up getting your arm sliced off by an old British

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:16.920
dude with a lightsaber.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:26.420
All kinds of crazy things might happen in
that world, but hey, crazy things happen in

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:27.700
our real world, too.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:31.640
It'd mostly be a hoot — unless, of course,
you end up working for the Empire.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:35.900
If you think your boss is always on your back
or intrudes too heavily upon your privacy,

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.720
just wait until you hear about the working
conditions of your average Imperial Stormtrooper

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.100
— and in particular, what really goes on
inside those helmets.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:47.400
Before we get into all of that, though, we
should get this out of the way: not just anybody

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:52.110
can fill out an application, undergo a background
check, and put on that iconic white armor,

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:55.600
all set for their first day of trying and
failing to shoot rebel soldiers.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.840
In the early days of the Empire, Stormtroopers
were recruited exclusively from the ranks

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:03.970
of the clones being produced on the planet
Kamino, an operation that was once overseen

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.140
by the deadly mercenary Jango Fett.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.960
This is why, in many of their cinematic appearances,
Stormtroopers all sound the same, are all

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.060
the same height, and are all left-handed.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:17.160
As time went on and the cloning operation
was eventually shuttered, the Empire began

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:21.850
to recruit Stormtroopers from its human population
— but they had to be rigorously tested for

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.120
absolute loyalty.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.960
They also presumably had to demonstrate with
their blaster rifles that they couldn't hit

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.560
the broad side of a barn.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.860
Once they were deemed compliant and loyal,
Stormtroopers were totally trusted to carry

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.480
out their orders and faithfully represent
the Empire without supervision.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:37.900
Or… scratch that.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:38.820
That's a lie.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.560
Actually, it was kind of the exact opposite
of that.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.020
Stormtroopers were required to stay in uniform
basically at all times while they were on

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.300
duty — which isn't fun, but also isn't the
bad part.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.840
While they were putting in the work for their
hard earned credits, they were also being

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:53.700
watched.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.280
In each Stormtrooper's helmet was a comm device
which broadcast everything that they said,

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:01.660
all the time, to the Empire — where it was
monitored and recorded.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:06.100
It was basically like being the target of
a super high-tech surveillance program, broadcasting

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.260
from your face, and always listening.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:12.680
These guys were getting eavesdropped on by
the boss every minute of every shift.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:17.260
This served two main purposes: ensuring they
remained productive and squelching potentially

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:18.560
rebellious speech.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.440
Now, to be sure, Stormtroopers' armor and
helmets had some pretty sweet features as

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:23.200
well.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.440
They were designed to dissipate energy, and
the helmets provided filtered air and an awesome

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.560
heads-up display, which compensated for their
limited field of vision — but apparently

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.960
did nothing to help them fire their blasters
more accurately.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:40.280
The fact that Stormtroopers' speech was constantly
monitored raises an interesting certainty:

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.120
there were millions of those guys, and somebody
had to be doing all of the monitoring.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:48.570
Perhaps this was done by specially programmed
droids, but we like to think that somewhere,

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:53.140
on some planet, was what basically amounted
to the biggest call center you've ever seen,

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.440
with cubicles stretching as far as the eye
can see.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.670
In each cubicle sat a loyal but underpaid
minion of the Empire, grinding through the

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:03.520
daily slog of poring over the audio records
of whatever Stormtrooper they'd been assigned

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:04.200
to.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.960
In the break room, they would boast to their
co-workers about how loyal and professional

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.140
"their guy" was.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:13.640
They'd make excuses for his poor marksmanship,
whispering under their breath that those heads-up

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.120
displays were made by some shady manufacturer
on the Outer Rim.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:22.080
And when "their guy" inevitably got blasted
into oblivion or carved up by a Jedi Knight,

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:25.910
they'd be allowed to take five and solemnly
reflect on his sacrifice while listening to

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:27.280
the Imperial March.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:31.000
Maybe living in the world of Star Wars wouldn't
be so awesome, after all.

